\title[Power-Sum GCD Factor Revelation]{Power-Sum GCD Factor Revelation \\ and Carmichael Periodicity}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{15 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
For a modulus $N$ and an exponent $k \ge 1$ let $F_N(k) = \sum_{a=1}^{N} a^{k}$ be the
$k$-th power sum of a complete residue system modulo $N$. We give a complete closed-form
determination of $g_N(k) = \gcd(F_N(k), N)$ for semiprime moduli, arbitrary squarefree
moduli, and odd prime powers. For $N = pq$ with $p \ne q$ prime and $k \ge 1$ we prove the
master formula $g_N(k) = (\,(p-1)\mid k\,?\,1:p\,)\cdot(\,(q-1)\mid k\,?\,1:q\,)$, from
which follow: an unconditional factor reveal $g_{pq}(p-1) = q$ for $p<q$; the fact that
the base-free power sum cannot suffer the ``bad base'' failure of Pollard's $p-1$ method,
made precise by exhibiting a base that is bad at every exponent; exact Carmichael
periodicity, with $\lambda(N)=\lcm(p-1,q-1)$ the least period and
$g_N(k)=1 \iff \lambda(N)\mid k$; the exact first-hit index $\min(p-1,q-1)<\sqrt N$; and an
exact count of revealing exponents per period. We show that the widely quoted recovery step
$p+q = N-\lambda(N)+1$ is false ($p=5$, $q=13$ is a counterexample), replace it by the
identity $\gcd(p-1,q-1)\cdot\lambda(N) + (p+q) = N+1$, and prove full factor recovery under
the guard $\gcd(p-1,q-1)=1$. For squarefree $N$ we obtain a bridge to Korselt's criterion:
$\gcd(F_N(N-1),N)=1$ exactly for Carmichael numbers. For an odd prime $p$ we prove
$\sum_{a<p^e} a^k \equiv -p^{e-1} \pmod{p^e}$ when $(p-1)\mid k$ and $\equiv 0$ otherwise,
the governing condition being $(p-1)\mid k$ rather than $\lambda(p^e)\mid k$. The cost to
the first hit is $O(N^{3/2})$, worse than trial division; the value of the development is
structural, isolating period finding as the sole locus of difficulty.
\end{abstract}

